Finance & AI Briefing

FIN-AI-2026-46 =C2=B7 16 November 2026 =C2=B7 Covering 9=E2=80=9315 November =
2026

1. WHAT CHANGED

Fineco is extending AI agents from employee assistance into the design of ban=
king processes and controls.

Reporting on 9 November described BOOST, a programme to redesign back-office =
operations, customer relationship management and internal controls using coll=
aborating AI agents. Fineco=E2=80=99s plans include continuous, predictive mo=
nitoring for audit, compliance and anti-money-laundering. These are programme=
 objectives, not evidence that autonomous controls have already replaced exis=
ting arrangements.

The structural change concerns where AI sits in the operating model. An assis=
tant helps an employee complete a task; collaborating agents can influence ho=
w work moves between departments, which exceptions receive attention and what=
 evidence reaches control functions.

That challenges the assumption that oversight can remain separate from workfl=
ow design. When agents generate both operational actions and monitoring infor=
mation, management needs to distinguish independent challenge from one automa=
ted process checking another. Financial and legal exposure then depends not o=
nly on answer quality, but also on delegated authority, missed exceptions and=
 the ability to reconstruct decisions.

EconomyUp: https://www.economyup.it/fintech/fineco-avvia-boost-cosi-gli-agent=
i-ai-ridisegnano-processi-e-controlli-interni/

2. MODEL RACE

Evaluating foundation model releases through enterprise cost, security, and d=
ata sovereignty.

BAIDU: ERNIE 5.5 Flash Preview

Released on 11 November through Qianfan=E2=80=99s API, ERNIE 5.5 Flash Previe=
w carried a promise of downloadable weights by 25 November. That promise does=
 not establish current self-hosting availability. The report provides no ente=
rprise-specific tariff, contractual residency terms or evidence of reduced ha=
llucinations in regulated banking tasks. Read more: https://pandaily.com/baid=
u-ernie-5-5-flash-preview-qianfan-api-weights-promised

LG AI RESEARCH: K-EXAONE 2.5 public API

LG made the existing K-EXAONE 2.5 API publicly available without charge, acco=
rding to reporting on 9 November. This changes access economics rather than e=
stablishing a new model release. Open-weight availability, self-hosting right=
s, enterprise service guarantees, data-residency commitments and banking-spec=
ific hallucination improvements were not established in the announcement. Rea=
d more: https://en.sedaily.com/finance/2026/11/09/lg-opens-national-ai-model-=
k-exaone-to-public-for-free

SAKANA AI: Sawara foundation-model programme

Reported on 13 November, Sawara is a planned foundation-model programme, not =
an available enterprise service. Pretraining is scheduled to begin in Decembe=
r, with Sawara Small weights targeted for spring 2027. API prices, deployment=
 jurisdictions, self-hosting terms and measured hallucination reductions rema=
in undisclosed at this announcement stage. Read more: https://alphasignal.ai/=
news/sakana-ai-builds-sawara-its-first-foundation-model-from-scratch

3. KEY DEVELOPMENTS

Verifiable industry milestones, major peer deployments, and regulatory framew=
ork shifts.

JPMORGAN CHASE: OpenAI coding and cybersecurity contract

JPMorgan signed a contract covering OpenAI=E2=80=99s Codex coding platform, c=
ybersecurity technology and broader AI deployments, according to OpenAI presi=
dent Greg Brockman. Reporting on 9 November disclosed neither contract value =
nor implementation scale. Brockman presented the agreement as an example of e=
nterprise customers=E2=80=99 growing contribution to OpenAI revenue. Read mor=
e: https://www.fstech.co.uk/fst/OpenAI_announces_partnership_with_JPMorgan_as=
_it_works_to_grow_enterprise_revenue.php

WELLS FARGO: Wealth-management AI Teammate

Wells Fargo=E2=80=99s wealth and investment management unit launched AI Teamm=
ate to help advisers and client associates retrieve institutional insights. R=
eporting on 10 November described information gathering and preparation of co=
nsolidated client presentations as target workflows. It did not disclose depl=
oyment expenditure, employee coverage or independently measured productivity =
gains. Read more: https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/8-question=
s-wells-fargo-wealth-172120912.html

BMO: Contact-centre support retains employee accountability

BMO described AI Agent Assist supporting contact-centre employees with credit=
-card transaction matching, policy retrieval and case preparation. Its 10 Nov=
ember disclosure states that employees remain accountable for reviewing gener=
ated information and making servicing decisions. The announcement documents w=
orkflow support rather than a transfer of decision responsibility to agents. =
Read more: https://finance.yahoo.com/technology/ai/articles/bmo-wins-five-omn=
i-awards-130000371.html

AIHK: Governance guidelines address connected agents

Guidelines reported on 12 November recommend three risk tiers, board oversigh=
t, an accountable executive and a central AI register. Agents connected to or=
ganisational systems receive at least an elevated-risk classification. These =
are governance recommendations, not a newly enacted banking mandate; outsourc=
ing does not transfer responsibility for an organisation=E2=80=99s use. Read =
more: https://fintechnews.hk/42109/ai/ai-governance-guidelines-aihk-hong-kong/

GROUP FOCUS

Published disclosures and strategic plans across BNP Paribas Group and entiti=
es.

- Group AI governance =E2=80=94 Coverage published on 9 November described ne=
arly 1,000 AI use cases underway at BNP Paribas, supported by a group-level f=
ramework with responsibilities distributed across businesses and functions. G=
roup CTO Jean-Michel Garcia identified customer service and employee effectiv=
eness as objectives. The figure describes an existing portfolio, not 1,000 la=
unches during the week. Read more: https://siliconangle.com/2026/11/09/ibm-bu=
ilds-hybrid-cloud-governance-advance-enterprise-ai-operating-model-ibmthink/

- Asset Management analytical tools =E2=80=94 A BNP Paribas Spain disclosure =
published on 10 November set out Asset Management=E2=80=99s 2026=E2=80=932030=
 sustainability strategy. Its plans include proprietary analytical tools and =
dashboards with AI-enabled capabilities, within a programme intended to stren=
gthen portfolio resilience and create client value. The disclosure describes =
strategic development rather than measured deployment outcomes. Read more: ht=
tps://www.bnpparibas.es/es/bnp-paribas-asset-management-lanza-su-estrategia-g=
lobal-de-sostenibilidad-2026-2030/
- Responsible and sustainable AI =E2=80=94 The same Asset Management strategy=
 includes leading industry initiatives on responsible and sustainable AI. Thi=
s is a separate policy commitment within the strategy, not an additional prod=
uct launch. The disclosure does not establish a dedicated AI investment budge=
t, quantified financial return or completed delivery against that commitment.=
 Read more: https://www.bnpparibas.es/es/bnp-paribas-asset-management-lanza-s=
u-estrategia-global-de-sostenibilidad-2026-2030/

4. CONTROL CONSIDERATIONS

- Whether collaborating agents have individually defined permissions, account=
able owners and escalation boundaries, including controls over the combined a=
ctions that become possible when agents exchange information across departmen=
tal workflows? (Technology, Operational Risk)

- Whether AI-enabled monitoring remains independent of the processes it asses=
ses, with testing for shared model failures, common data errors and exception=
s that disappear between operational and oversight systems? (Compliance, Inte=
rnal Audit)
- Whether coding and cybersecurity contracts identify permitted access to rep=
ositories, credentials and production systems, alongside approval requirement=
s for generated code, vulnerability testing and automated remediation activit=
ies? (Engineering, Security)

- Whether adviser-facing assistants distinguish approved research from genera=
ted interpretation, preserve source dates and document human review before in=
formation enters client presentations, recommendations or other customer comm=
unications? (Wealth Management, Conduct)
- Whether contact-centre employees can challenge AI-generated transaction mat=
ches and policy interpretations, with sufficient source evidence, escalation =
capacity and time to exercise the responsibility formally assigned to them? (=
Customer Operations, Compliance)

- Whether free or preview APIs have documented limits, contractual protection=
s and exit arrangements, with business cases separating temporary access pric=
es from production costs, service guarantees and validation expenditure? (Pro=
curement, Finance)
- Whether announced open-weight releases are distinguished from weights curre=
ntly available under usable licences, and whether self-hosting assessments co=
ver telemetry, maintenance access, monitoring services and downstream data tr=
ansfers? (Architecture, Privacy)

- Whether AI-related credit exposure is aggregated across developers, infrast=
ructure operators, power suppliers and investment vehicles, including depende=
ncies on common customers, refinancing assumptions and contracts supporting p=
rojected revenues? (Credit Risk, Treasury)

